

First, add a new Widget Control section to the Admin Console, alongside the existing Job Access and User Accounts sections, using the same full-view-with-back-button pattern already used for those groups. It should list every widget currently present on the dashboard, generated dynamically from whatever widgets are actually registered rather than a hardcoded list, so any widget added in future shows up here automatically. For each widget, provide a single enable or disable toggle for the widget as a whole, using the same shared toggle component already used for Job Access, plus an expandable list of the applications that widget currently displays, each with its own enable or disable toggle, so one specific application can be hidden from a widget without disabling the widget for every other application. When a widget, or one application within a widget, is disabled, the dashboard's auto-refresh timer and manual refresh button must both skip triggering the underlying job for that widget or application, so it does not run in the background. This must have no effect anywhere else in the system: the same underlying job run directly from the Jobs view or Console view must keep working exactly as it does today. A disabled widget should be hidden from the dashboard entirely rather than shown empty, loading, or in an error state, and a disabled application inside an otherwise enabled widget should have just that application's entry hidden while the widget keeps showing normal data for its other enabled applications. Toggle changes must take effect immediately, on the very next auto-refresh cycle or manual refresh, with no app restart needed. Keep this admin-only and reuse the same storage and config pattern already used for the per-job enable/disable and per-application override behavior in Job Access, rather than building a separate parallel mechanism.

Second, fix the shared scrollbar component for real. The popover that lists all currently running jobs is scrollable, and its scrollbar is still rendering as the plain default browser scrollbar instead of the shared pill-shaped, borderless, hover-revealed, click-and-drag scrollbar used elsewhere in the app. A previous fix reported that duplicate scrollbar style definitions were found and removed, but this popover still shows the old scrollbar, so the underlying cause was not actually resolved. Do a full audit of every scrollable container in the app, including this popover, and verify each one is actually applying the shared scrollbar component or class at runtime, not just that duplicate CSS was removed from the codebase elsewhere. If this popover renders through a portal, overlay, or a separate UI primitive that sits outside the app's normal stylesheet scope, apply the shared scrollbar styling there explicitly, since that would explain why the earlier cleanup did not fix this instance.

Third, two header layout fixes. On the Dashboard tab, the Dashboard, Jobs, Console tab switcher and the Refresh button, together with the last-updated timestamp beneath it, currently wrap onto two separate lines even though there is enough horizontal space for both to sit in one row. Put them on the same row, tab switcher on the left and the refresh control on the right, and only let them wrap onto separate lines if the viewport is genuinely too narrow to fit both. In the Resource Utilization widget, the widget title and the mode toggle next to it are also currently stacked on two lines for the same reason. Put the title and the toggle on the same row as well, title on the left and toggle on the right, matching the same one-line header pattern as the dashboard fix above.
